ef 1063 #edb/ref 17592186092313 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093356 #edb/ref 1064 "Any curve defined using polar coordinates $(r(\\theta), \\theta)$ is a parametric curve in the parameter $\\theta.$ For this reason, we can use the formula for differentiating parametric curves to compute the derivative of a polar curve:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(\\theta)}{x'(\\theta)}\n$$\n\nAs an example, suppose we want to find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}$ for the polar curve $r = \\sin4\\theta,$ shown below.\n\n![](images/5a/5a0d25e54c2d688341f4bc1d0046cf485008cd0e2556d6bf2b9c8ebf8c20b0e2.png)\n\nFirst, recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx(\\theta) = r \\cos \\theta, \\qquad y(\\theta) = r \\sin \\theta.\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that $r=\\sin 4\\theta$ for this particular curve, we get\n\n$$\nx(\\theta) = \\sin 4\\theta \\cos\\theta, \\qquad y(\\theta) =\\sin4\\theta \\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nTo compute our derivative, we first need to calculate $x'(\\theta)$ and $y'(\\theta)\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n- First, we compute the derivative of $x$ with respect to $\\theta$ using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{'}(\\theta ) & =(\\operatorname{sin}4\\theta \\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{'} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}4\\theta )^{'}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}4\\theta \\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{'} \\\\  & =(4\\operatorname{cos}4\\theta )\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}4\\theta \\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}\\theta ) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}4\\theta \\operatorname{cos}\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}4\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute the derivative of $y$ with respect to $\\theta$ using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'}(\\theta ) & =(\\operatorname{sin}4\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{'} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}4\\theta )^{'}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}4\\theta \\cdot (\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{'} \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}4\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}4\\theta \\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}\\theta ) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}4\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}4\\theta \\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{'}(\\theta )}{{x}^{'}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\operatorname{cos}4\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}4\\theta \\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{4\\operatorname{cos}4\\theta \\operatorname{cos}\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}4\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093356 #edb/ref 1065 1119 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093356 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "68089c07-eb7d-5103-9642-9bfffd81f0eb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093356 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093428 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we know the position of a point $P(r, \\theta)$ in polar coordinates, and we want to convert it into Cartesian coordinates.\n\nTo do this, we can draw a right triangle with legs $x$ and $y$ and hypotenuse $r.$\n\n![](images/20/205b3684042b0ab0261a4d56800cd89a10a7816f8022befcf6c664337f834380.png)\n\nComputing the trigonometric ratios for the triangle above and solving for $x$ and $y,$ we get the following formulas:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  & =\\frac{x}{r}\\, & \\Rightarrow  & \\, & x & =r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta , \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{r}\\, & \\Rightarrow  & \\, & y & =r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's use these formulas to write the point $P(2, 60^\\circ)$ in Cartesian coordinates. Here, we have $r=2$ and $\\theta =60^\\circ.$ So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2\\operatorname{cos}{60}^{∘}=2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=1, \\\\ y & =2\\operatorname{sin}{60}^{∘}=2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}=\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P$ can be written in Cartesian coordinates as $P(1, \\sqrt{3}).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093428 #edb/ref 1065 3505 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093428 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "0c11e98f-8b92-5ed1-bfd7-cca35a6dd373" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093428 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096756 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049533 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096756 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050571 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096756 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050819 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096756 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186096948 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096756 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186097406 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096756 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247047 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096756 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247079 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096756 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247097 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096756 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297442 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096756 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186319002 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096756 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186319003 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096756 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186319004 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096756 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186319005 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096756 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186332670 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096756 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186332916 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096756 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186332917 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096756 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362388 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096756 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186544829 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096756 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating the Area Bounded Below a Curve and Above the X-Axis" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096756 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186096581 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096756 #edb/ref 1042 