ef 17592186185006 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184419 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049327 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184419 #edb/ref 1050 1260.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184419 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "af91633b-288c-52bd-a641-7172621608f6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184944 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186185001 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184944 #edb/ref 1061 642669 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184944 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a0ead2f8-7726-5a22-ad4d-582c7b4b8968" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184944 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186184177 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185001 #edb/ref 1061 642672 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185001 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e6c2d34a-6683-50c4-a82e-b71e985d4ed8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185001 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186184215 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185006 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186184944 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185006 #edb/ref 1061 642668 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185006 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a1e51e4b-71cf-5749-aae1-c001b3a5a59b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185006 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186185342 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185342 #edb/ref 1064 "![](images/a0/a0b4ad67ea80f6d6eec3a15b3df18d551d84263c7424405588bd15162ef4e7c2.png)\n\nThe polar curve shown above is defined as\n\n$$\nr(\\theta) = 4 - 3\\cos \\theta, \\qquad 0\\leq \\theta < 2\\pi.\n$$\n\nWe have $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ at the point $P.$ The tangent to the curve at $P$ is shown in the diagram. \n\nTo find the equation of the tangent line to the curve at $P,$ we follow four steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculate the derivative $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}.$\n\nUsing techniques that we've previously discussed, we can show that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta )=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta ,\\,{x}^{′}(\\theta )=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the slope of the tangent at $P.$\n\nThe slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})}{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )}{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{0+3}{0-4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P.$\n\nWe compute the $xy$-coordinates of $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =(4-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =(4-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =0 \\\\ {y}_{1} & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =(4-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =(4-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $\\left( 0, 4 \\right).$\n\n**Step 4**: Using point-slope form, find the equation of the tangent.\n\nWe find the equation of the tangent as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-4 & =-\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot (x-0) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{3}{4}x+4\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185342 #edb/ref 1065 6186 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185342 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "febc7b49-9662-5e78-a7a5-cbe9b1f596ba" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185342 #edb/ref 1067 