ng gives\n\n$$\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}u}=-\\sin u,\\qquad \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\cos x.$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule:\n\n$$f'(x)=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=-\\sin(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + \\frac{3 \\pi}{4})\\cos x.$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0$ gives $f'(0)=-\\sin(\\frac{3 \\pi}{4})=- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$.\n\nFinally, use the point-slope equation:\n\n$$y-\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)=\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)(x-0),\\qquad y=- \\frac{\\sqrt{2} x}{2} - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186484800 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186484796 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186484800 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186484800 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y=\\cos{\\left(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + \\frac{\\pi}{6} \\right)}$ at the point $\\left(0,\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)$.\n\nWrite the right-hand side of the equation in terms of $x$.\n\n$y=$ {{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186484800 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "53218be1-6a3c-5560-9338-ef81bd9ab181" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186484800 #edb/ref 1074 "First, the slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=0$ is given by $f'(0)$.\n\nWe calculate $f'(x)$ using the chain rule. First, express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$f=\\cos u,\\qquad u=\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + \\frac{\\pi}{6}.$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}u}=-\\sin u,\\qquad \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\cos x.$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule:\n\n$$f'(x)=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=-\\sin(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + \\frac{\\pi}{6})\\cos x.$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0$ gives $f'(0)=-\\sin(\\frac{\\pi}{6})=- \\frac{1}{2}$.\n\nFinally, use the point-slope equation:\n\n$$y-\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)=\\left(- \\frac{1}{2}\\right)(x-0),\\qquad y=- \\frac{x}{2} + \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443