(0)=-\\sin(\\frac{2 \\pi}{3})=- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$.\n\nFinally, use the point-slope equation:\n\n$$y-\\left(- \\frac{1}{2}\\right)=\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)(x-0),\\qquad y=- \\frac{\\sqrt{3} x}{2} - \\frac{1}{2}.$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186490484 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490495 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186490484 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186490484 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $f(x)={sin}^{4}⁡(x),$ we have\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186490484 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "140dd45d-8107-5dae-9564-2bd6cb1a4f7b" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186490484 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we express\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))^{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, we see that $f(x)$ can be expressed as the composite function\n\n$$\nf={u}^{4},\\,u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=4{u}^{3},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =4{u}^{3}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) \\\\  & =4{sin}^{3}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186490486 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490493 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186490486 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186490486 #edb/ref 1071 "Find ${f}^{′}(x)$ when $f(x)={cos}^{6}⁡(x).$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186490486 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1da4f94b-c6e6-57bb-95c9-efc33159af10" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186490486 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we express\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{6}.\n$$\n\nNow, we see that $f(x)$ can be expressed as the composite function\n\n$$\nf={u}^{6},\\,u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=6{u}^{5}\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =6{u}^{5}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =-6{u}^{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =-6(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =-6{cos}^{5}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186490490 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490492 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186490490 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186490490 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $f(x)=-2{cos}^{6}⁡(x),$ we have\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186490490 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "65409563-6aa3-541a-86d5-30b4fe746231" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186490490 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we express\n\n$$\nf(x)=-2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))^{6}.\n$$\n\nNow, we see that $f(x)$ can be expressed as the composite function\n\n$$\nf=-2{u}^{6},\\,u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=-12{u}^{5},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =-12{u}^{5}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)) \\\\  & =12{cos}^{5}⁡(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186490497 #edb/ref 1168 "generate/scratch/17592186064283.py" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186490497 #edb/ref 1169 #uuid "56b53014-1c7c-5126-a574-20ed217b6cb5" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186491422 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186491439 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186491422 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186491422 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y=12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-1)$ at the point $(1+\\pi ,-12).$\n\n{{selection}}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186491422 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a8a0d3c1-7c1b-5f34-8315-2483a617ccdf" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186491422 #edb/ref 1074 "First, the slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=1+\\pi $ is given by ${f}^{′}(1+\\pi ).$\n\nWe will calculate ${f}^{′}(x)$ using the chain rule. First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=12\\operatorname{cos}⁡u,\\,u=x-1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=1+\\pi ,$ we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}m={f}^{′}(1+\\pi ) & =-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1+\\pi -1) \\\\  & =-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the equation of the tangent at the point $(1+\\pi ,-12):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-12) & =0(x-(1+\\pi )) \\\\ y+12 & =0 \\\\ y & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186491425 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186491417 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186491425 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186491425 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-1)$ at the point $(1+\\pi ,-3).$\n\n{{selection}}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186491425 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cf807996-e431-5716-aed7-89d8d85c588a" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186491425 #edb/ref 1074 "First, the slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=1+\\pi $ is given by ${f}^{′}(1+\\pi ).$\n\nWe will calculate ${f}^{′}(x)$ using the chain rule. First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡u,\\,u=x-1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=1+\\pi ,$ we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}m={f}^{′}(1+\\pi ) & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1+\\pi -1) \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the equation of the tangent at the point $(1+\\pi ,-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-3) & =0(x-(1+\\pi )) \\\\ y+3 & =0 \\\\ y & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186491432 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186491409 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186491432 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186491432 #edb/ref 1071 "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12-2x)-6{x}^{3}.$\n\n{{selection}}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186491432 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "edc669f2-8811-573d-b7ea-ebf03a0592e1" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186491432 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $g(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12-2x)$ and $h(x)=-6{x}^{3}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =g(x)+h(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we differentiate $g(x).$ We express $g(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12-2x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ng=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u,\\,u=12-2x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}u}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-2.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡u \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12-2x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate $h(x)=-6{x}^{3}.$ \n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x}=-18{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)={g}^{′}(x)+{h}^{′}(x)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12-2x)-18{x}^{2}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186491434 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186491421 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186491434 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186491434 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the slope $m$ of $y =\\sin \\left(\\pi \\left(8x^{2} + 2x - 2\\right)\\right)$ at $x = 1.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186491434 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "efb4aba1-7652-55ac-8883-4dc872ffd09b" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186491434 #edb/ref 1074 "The slope of $y = f(x)$ at $x=1$ is given by $f'(1).$\n\nWe will calculate $f'(x)$ using the chain rule. First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n\\[\ny = \\sin{u}, \\qquad u = \\pi \\left(8x^{2} +2x - 2\\right).\n\\]\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\\[\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = \\cos{u}, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = \\pi (16x + 2).\n\\]\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n\\begin{align*}\nf'(x) &= \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x}\\\\[5pt]\n\n&=\\cos u \\cdot \\pi (16x+2)\\\\[5pt]\n\n&=\\pi (16x+2)\\cos u\\\\[5pt]\n\n&=\\pi (16x+2)\\cos \\left(  \\pi \\left(8x^{2} +2x - 2\\right) \\right).\n\\end{align*}\n\nEvaluating at $x=1,$ we find\n\n\\begin{align}\nf'(1) & =\\pi (16\\cdot1+2)\\cos \\left(  \\pi \\left(8\\cdot1^{2} +2\\cdot1 - 2\\right) \\right) \\\\[3pt]\n\n& =18\\pi \\cos(8\\pi) \\\\[3pt]\n&= 18\\pi \\cdot (1) \\\\[3pt]\n&= 18 \\pi\n\\end{align}\n\nTherefore, the slope $m$ of $y = f(x)$ at $x = 1$ is \n\\[\nm = f'(1) = \\bbox[4pt, border: 1px solid lightgray]{18 \\pi}.\n\\]" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186491436 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186491418 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186491436 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186491436 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y=11\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-1)$ at the point $(1+\\pi ,-11).$\n\n{{selection}}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592