x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ x & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({315}^{∘}) \\\\ x & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({315}^{∘}) \\\\ y & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ y & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(1,-1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186535354 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186535381 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186535354 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186535354 #edb/ref 1071 "The point $P$ has polar coordinates $(6\\sqrt{2},{45}^{∘}).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186535354 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2369bcf6-10b1-57c2-a3aa-fa5b4cd63d5d" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186535354 #edb/ref 1074 "To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ x & =6\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({45}^{∘}) \\\\ x & =6\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ x & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y & =6\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({45}^{∘}) \\\\ y & =6\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ y & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(6,6).$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186535355 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186535351 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186535355 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186535390 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186535355 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186535355 #edb/ref 1071 "If the point $P$ has polar coordinates $(\\sqrt{3